<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomorie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anchialos, Anchialus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anchialus, now Pomorie, is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, founded in antiquity as a Greek colony of Apollonia. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Byzantine fortress on the frontier with Bulgaria and changed hands repeatedly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> won a battle near it in 763, and Simeon I destroyed a Byzantine army by the nearby river Achelous in 917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomorie.jpg" alt="Pomorie" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boby Dimitrov from Sofia, Bulgaria · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Town_of_Pomorie_aerial_Boby_Dimitrov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stands on a narrow rocky peninsula that extends 3.5 km into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> on the north-west coast of Burgas Bay, in Burgas Province. The sea surrounds it on the south, east and north-east, and Lake Pomorie, an ultrasaline lagoon and the northernmost of the Burgas Lakes, lies to the north. Only to the west-north-west does it join the Pomorie field, part of the Burgas lowland. The climate is humid subtropical, with winters milder than in the inland part of the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Anchialos comes from Ancient Greek words meaning near and sea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> called the town Tuthon. It was possibly founded in the 5th or 4th century BCE as a colony of Apollonia, today Sozopol, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s Geographica mentions it as a small town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> in 29-28 BCE. Anchialos was formally proclaimed a city under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and prospered in the 2nd and 3rd centuries under the Severan Dynasty as the most important import and export station of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p>Barbarian invasions ended this prosperity in the middle of the 3rd century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> briefly captured the city around 270. Its closeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> later made it a key food supply centre. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> formed in 681, Anchialos became a frontier fortress: Bulgar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervel">Khan Tervel</a> defeated the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> near it in 708, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Khan Krum</a> took it in 812 and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and Bulgars there, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> restored their control in 864. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> held it again until 971, when Byzantium reconquered it and kept it for two centuries.</p><p>The town submitted to Ottoman rule in 1453, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and as Ahyolu became the centre of a kaza and of an eparchy of the Patriarchate of Constantinople. Two patriarchs of Constantinople came from it. Anchialos was liberated from Ottoman rule on 27 January 1878 and joined Eastern Rumelia, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1885. In July 1906 the town was burnt down and over 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> were murdered by Bulgarian refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, and European diplomacy compared the destruction to the anti-Jewish pogroms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It was renamed Pomorie in 1934 or 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pomorie is a seaside resort and the administrative centre of Pomorie Municipality, with a population of 13,926 at the 2020 count. It is known for wine and salt production, and its landmarks include a Salt Museum, a municipal museum and gallery, an ancient Thracian beehive tomb of the 3rd century CE, the Church of the Transfiguration of God (1765) and St George's Monastery (1856).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pomorie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ports such as Varna, Mesembria (Nesebar) and <b>Anchialos</b> linked the country to wider trade, and Ivan Asen II granted merchants from Ragusa (Dubrovnik) a charter allowing them to trade freely in his lands; Venetians and Genoese became prominent in the fourteenth century, when the tsars issued silver coinage…</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantine Emperor Constantine V defeated the Bulgarian khan Telets near <b>Anchialus</b> on the Black Sea coast, one of the greatest victories of his long campaigns against Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-30">30 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
